in": -11.4, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.72, "baseline_margin": -6.11, "analyst_adjustment": 1.11, "key_factors": ["R+6 district with incumbent Mike Flood running for a third full term", "Expert consensus of Likely/Solid R across ten raters", "Recent Democratic-sponsored poll shows a tied race, but only one fresh poll", "Flood holds a large fundraising edge ($1.56M cash vs $291K)"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a Republican incumbent in an R+6 district with a big money advantage, and raters overwhelmingly call it Likely or Solid R. A September Democratic-sponsored poll showing a one-point race and a contested debate season justify softening the expected margin, but not enough to move it out of Likely R.", "watch": "Any additional independent polling confirming the tied September survey, or significant outside spending against Flood, would move this toward Tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Mike Flood (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Republican Mike Flood is seeking a third full term in Nebraska's 1st District, a seat that leans about six points Republican. He faces Democrat Chris Backemeyer, plus Libertarian and independent candidates, in a race that has drawn genuine attention including a well-covered debate over Iran, tariffs, Medicaid and agriculture.\n\nFlood holds a decisive financial edge, and ten expert ratings cluster at Likely or Solid R, with prediction markets pricing his chances near 80 percent. Still, a September Democratic-sponsored poll of likely voters showed the race tied, and Backemeyer has been competitive enough to keep this from being called Safe R. The evidence points to a Flood win with a modest but not overwhelming margin.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1466, "margin": -6.26}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1237, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1206, "margin": -8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2177, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.